WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY: Finding
your data assets is one thing.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.910
Managing both your technical
and business metadata while

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.890
maintaining proper
security requirements

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.080
is a whole other beast.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.630
Luckily, Data Catalog can help.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.345
Stay tuned as we cover how
to add schematized tags

00:00:15.345 --> 00:00:17.760
to your data assets
as well as show you

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.310
how integration with
Cloud Identity and Access

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:25.300
Management or Cloud IAM provides
ACL controls on your metadata.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:37.970
Data Catalog is a fully managed
and scalable data discovery

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:41.450
and metadata management service
that empowers organizations

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:44.360
to quickly discover,
understand, and manage

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.190
all their data in Google Cloud.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.110
I am Anita Kibunguchy.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.930
And today, we'll be tackling
how to understand your data

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:55.280
assets with schematized tags
in a safe and secure way.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:58.160
Just like sticky notes,
Data Catalog tags

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.410
act as annotations.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:03.410
Whereas other data catalogs
capture tags a simple text

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:06.500
strings, Data Catalog
captures business metadata

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.830
in a schematized
format via tags.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:12.650
These tags can be created
through the UI as well

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.990
as custom APIs.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:19.970
Users can now define a business
tag as string, double, Boolean,

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:21.650
or enumerated-- enum--

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:24.903
making it easy to catalog
and find data assets.

00:01:24.903 --> 00:01:27.320
So you've heard us talk a lot
about technical and business

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.370
metadata.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.350
So what do we mean
by these terms?

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.940
Technical metadata
is data that's

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:36.140
already saved in GCP services,
such as table names, column

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.050
descriptions, and date created.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.200
This data is automatically
ingested from the data source

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.980
into Data Catalog without
any involvement on your part.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.340
For example, if you add
a new table to BigQuery,

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.190
it will show up in Data
Catalog in a matter of seconds.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.290
On the other hand,
business metadata

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:57.410
are tags that provide additional
business information that

00:01:57.410 --> 00:01:59.840
is valuable to a customer.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.010
This could include
PII, delete-by dates,

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.830
business logic,
data quality scores,

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.530
as well as governance tags.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:12.290
By adding business
metadata, data assets

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:15.710
become more searchable
and usable for your team.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.210
Let's take a look
at how you can add

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:20.720
rich information to your data
assets with tag templates.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.860
On the Data Catalog home screen,
scroll to Tag Template section

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:28.280
and click on Create
a tag template.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.800
Here, you can define
the template ID,

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.260
display name, as well
as add attributes

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.630
and define their type.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:39.320
Each attribute type can be
string, double, Boolean,

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.120
or enum.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.710
Once you've added
all your attributes,

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:46.130
click on Save Template.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:49.100
Creating a tag template can
also be done programmatically

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.470
using custom APIs.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:56.420
Let's learn more about
an existing tag template.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:59.900
On the Data Catalog home screen,
scroll to the Tag Template

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.730
section and click on
Explore tag templates.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:08.720
We'll look at the Data
Governance tag templates.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.940
This template has tags to
help the data governance

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:14.300
team certify specific
data assets for use.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:19.190
It also lets approved users know
which data assets they can use.

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:23.110
You'll notice that each
attribute has a type as well.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:27.080
For data classification,
the type is enum.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:28.970
From there, the
data governance team

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:32.990
can now define whether the data
asset is public, sensitive,

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:36.230
confidential, or
regulatory, and can then

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:39.210
apply the applicable
controls around it.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:42.290
Now that you've explored
a tag template, let's look

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.870
at how the integration
with IAM helps with access

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:46.730
control of your data assets.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.070
Instead of having to set
separate permissions or ACLs

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:52.190
to register data
assets, Data Catalog

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:55.280
auto-ingests tactical metadata
and honors existing source

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:55.970
ACLs.

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:58.730
This means that if a user
already has read access

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:01.310
to all data assets
in BigQuery, then

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:04.400
they'll be able to discover and
have read access to those data

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.100
assets in Data Catalog.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:10.790
Similarly, this IAM integration
allows further access controls

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:13.480
to be set.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.329
With Data Catalog, you
can set read access,

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:19.810
metadata read-only access, and
no read or no metadata read

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:21.339
access.

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:23.650
Depending on these
permission settings,

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:25.840
users' search results
and access can

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.040
be controlled, keeping highly
sensitive data limited to those

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:31.837
with the necessary access.

00:04:31.837 --> 00:04:34.420
Thanks for watching, and please
check out all our Data Catalog

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.550
videos and visit our
website to learn more.

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:39.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

